{"author":{"id":"a3a282966ced55effb4560271fcd98e13241af24ea76b00ec76a0f18154bc8ee"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790266488,"nonce":"+fp3YxhyekMP2senogCSwI0cZgQ=","message":"Revised 2026-09-24 (Luis): this task is the blocking terminal backend of `work.view.*`, which is also what `git work view \u003ckind\u003e` runs on a TTY. A view call is an atomic capability: it renders, handles its own interaction, writes its own edits through the host, and does not return until the user quits, so Starlark is paused inside the call and the renderer may call back into the script synchronously — there is no concurrency in the language and no scheduler to build. `items` arrives either as a list (static) or as a provider function, `lambda: work.issue.list(q)`, which the renderer calls again after its own edits and whenever the ref watcher (63c68d1) reports a change; that is how a board stays live without re-running the flow, and it replaces \"re-run the flow\" from the 2026-09-23 note. The return value is the user's answer, not a spec: a board returns None and a list with `pick=True` returns the chosen item, which is what `flow pick` (9a24c8e) needs. Callbacks (`on_change(item, field, old, new)` owning the write, `on_select(item)` supplying the return value) are a probable but deferred feature — sketch only, do not build them into this renderer yet. Two panes at once is a composite view, `work.view.split(...)`, not two view calls. A kind this backend does not implement fails naming itself. Design: doc/design/config-entity.md E9.","files":null}]}